{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RZGwwDRZp9MotZJ6b26mSg","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Excellent. Now I have a comprehensive view of the codebase. Let me compile my findings on architectural friction points.\n\n## Architectural Friction Survey: Almanac Codebase\n\nBased on my thorough analysis of the Almanac loop engine, here are the **eight priority friction points** where shallow modules, duplicated patterns, or leaky seams invite reconvergence of complexity:\n\n### 1. **Flag-parsing boilerplate across all three loop adapters** (MEDIUM severity)\n**Location:** `lib/loops/ralph.sh:50–65`, `lib/loops/harden.sh:46–61`, `lib/loops/converge.sh:58–76`\n\n**Symptom:** Each adapter's `launch` verb begins with identical `while`/`case` flag parsing: iterate args, normalize provider to lowercase, validate with `[ -n \"$X\" ] || _die`, etc. The patterns are so congruent that adding a fourth loop will tempt copy-paste.\n\n**Deletion test:** Remove the boilerplate → the logic reconverges into launcher code (one switch-statement per loop), bloating `lib/loop-launcher.sh`.\n\n**Candidate deepening:** A shared `_almanac_launch_parse_common_flags` helper in `loop-launcher.sh` that normalizes provider, handles `--help`/`--yes`, and returns a structured dict/array of parsed fields. Each adapter would call it once and then do only loop-specific prompts.\n\n---\n\n### 2. **`new_run_argv` kv-pair parsing duplicated in harden/converge adapters** (LOW-MEDIUM severity)\n**Location:** `lib/loops/harden.sh:97–110`, `lib/loops/converge.sh:167–188`\n\n**Symptom:** Both adapters implement identical kv-pair iteration:\n```bash\nfor kv in \"$@\"; do\n  key=\"${kv%%=*}\"; val=\"${kv#*=}\"\n  case \"$key\" in ...\n```\nRalph doesn't (because it already handles the common provider/model/effort fields via its own loop), but harden/converge repeat this exact pattern with different `case` branches.\n\n**Deletion test:** Remove the duplication → the loop-agnostic hub code in `lib/run.sh` would have to handle kv pair unpacking itself, losing the adapter's ability to validate schema.\n\n**Candidate deepening:** A shared `_almanac_adapter_parse_kv_pairs` in `loops.sh` that yields name-indexed fields, so adapters just validate and compose argv/env without re-parsing.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Role-field resolution duplicated in harden-core and converge-core** (LOW severity)\n**Location:** `lib/harden-core.sh:61–68` (abbreviated), `lib/converge-core.sh:56–60`\n\n**Symptom:** Both define a `_role_field` function with identical shape:\n```bash\n<loop>_role_field() {\n  <loop>_role … | awk -F'\\t' -v k=\"$field\" '$1 == k { v = $2 } END { print v }'\n}\n```\nThe awk pipeline is identical; only the role resolver differs. If a third loop needs roles, this pattern repeats.\n\n**Deletion test:** Remove the helpers → each consumer (reviewer/fixer invocation) would parse the TSV directly, losing the single indirection.\n\n**Candidate deepening:** A generic `almanac_loop_role_field` in `role.sh` that consumes the output of any role resolver. It's already exported from `role.sh` for consumers; harden/converge just need to not redefine it locally.\n\n---\n\n### 4. **Hub flag-parsing loses loop-agnosticism** (MEDIUM severity)\n**Location:** `cmd/hub.sh:47–149` (esp. lines 84–93, 127–147)\n\n**Symptom:** The hub's interactive menu hardcodes the action menu (watch/stop/steer/back) and the run selection is generic, BUT the `--new`, `--resume`, and `--clone` paths are thin dispatchers that DO branch on loop type implicitly: the `new_run_argv`/`new_run_env` composer contract is loop-agnostic, but the hub has to CALL it correctly. If a loop redefines what \"resume\" means (e.g., harden's no-auto-confirm `launch_backed` contract), the hub must know to honor it. This is a seam leak: the hub is not truly loop-agnostic because it resolves the adapter verbs, not the launchers.\n\n**Deletion test:** Remove the hub's `--resume`/`--clone` logic → each loop would need its own hub variant.\n\n**Candidate deepening:** The adapter's `launch_backed` verb is already the right deepening, but the hub doesn't use it consistently. Converge/harden never have the suffix appended because `launch_backed` is undefined; the hub should dispatch that check once and apply the result unconditionally.\n\n---\n\n### 5. **Shared UI seam called identically by three separate launcher paths** (LOW severity)\n**Location:** `cmd/ralph.sh`, `cmd/harden.sh`, `cmd/hub.sh` (and others)\n\n**Symptom:** The launcher (`lib/loop-launcher.sh`) sources the UI seam and offers the field collectors; every adapter uses them identically. The entry points then each source `loop-launcher.sh` again (ralph.sh, harden.sh inline; hub.sh transitively). This is not a duplication bug — it's correct idempotent sourcing — but it's a surprising architectural point: the UI seam lives in the launcher's scope, not in a shared library where a new command might independently source it. If a future command needs the UI but not the launcher (e.g., a direct harden runner), it would have to source launcher.sh, importing the full field-collector apparatus as well.\n\n**Deletion test:** Move UI field collectors to `ui.sh` → the launcher imports them, and new commands can source UI directly. No reconvergence, just better factorization.\n\n**Candidate deepening:** Extract `_almanac_launch_*` and `almanac_loop_launch_summary` from `loop-launcher.sh` into a new `lib/launcher-helpers.sh` or fold them into `loop-launcher.sh`'s init (sourced before adapters) so they're not in the caller's scope.\n\n---\n\n### 6. **Worker and run-status TSV record engines share a generic deep module but lack a single schema registry** (LOW severity)\n**Location:** `lib/run.sh:41–45` (run fields), `lib/worker.sh:29–34` (worker fields)\n\n**Symptom:** Both use the same `almanac_loop_tsv_record_set` engine, which is perfect — the deepening worked. However, the two schema definitions live in separate files and are never cross-checked. A consumer that reads both (e.g., a dashboard) must know BOTH field lists. The hub's read views in `lib/run.sh` hardcode run-status field names; if a field is added to `almanac_loop_record_fields`, the hub doesn't automatically see it.\n\n**Deletion test:** Remove the generic engine → each would need its own set/get pair, and the two schemas would diverge faster.\n\n**Candidate deepening:** Not a new module, but a documented registry: a comments-only section in `run.sh` that lists both `almanac_loop_record_fields` AND `almanac_loop_worker_record_fields` with their semantic meaning, so additions are captured in one place and tests can compare.\n\n---\n\n### 7. **Provider adapter contract verification missing; discovery is silent** (LOW-MEDIUM severity)\n**Location:** `lib/agent.sh:35–43` (discovery), tests/test-agent.sh (provider contract tested via fake mocks only)\n\n**Symptom:** When a provider adapter is added at `lib/providers/<name>.sh`, there's no check that it implements the full contract (available, models, efforts, display, argv, filter, extract). If an adapter omits `extract`, the run shapes will crash at call time. The test suite tests the discovery mechanism and a few canonical calls, but doesn't generically verify that every discovered adapter is valid.\n\n**Deletion test:** Remove the silent contract → a malformed adapter would silently fail at runtime instead of during discovery/startup.\n\n**Candidate deepening:** A `_almanac_provider_validate` verb that each provider must implement (returns 0 or dies with a message). Called once after discovery so startup fails fast on a misconfigured adapter.\n\n---\n\n### 8. **Loop adapter `status_to_opts` is untested; round-trip composition via verbs is implicit** (LOW severity)\n**Location:** `lib/loops/ralph.sh:167–188`, `lib/loops/harden.sh:139–155`, `lib/loops/converge.sh:228–250` + invoked by `cmd/hub.sh:219–234` (in the resumed version)\n\n**Symptom:** Each adapter defines `status_to_opts`, which inverts `new_run_argv`/`new_run_env` from a status file. The round-trip is a deep contract (run → status → new run must be identical) but it's tested implicitly: the hub's `--resume` uses it, but there's no dedicated test that `status_to_opts` output feeds back through the new-run composers and reconstructs the original run. If the schema drifts (e.g., a field is added to the status record but not to `status_to_opts`), the test suite won't catch it.\n\n**Deletion test:** Remove the verbs → the hub would have to re-parse status.tsv fields directly, losing the adapter's ownership of the schema.\n\n**Candidate deepening:** A three-phase test for each adapter: (1) launch a run with known config, (2) read its status, (3) call `status_to_opts`, (4) feed to `new_run_argv`/`new_run_env`, (5) assert the output matches the input config (verbatim round-trip). Protects against schema drift and signals when the adapter is incomplete.\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\n**Ranked by reconvergence risk (severity if deleted):**\n1. **Flag-parsing boilerplate** (if deleted, launcher bloats)\n2. **Hub's implicit loop-type branching** (forces loop-specific hub variants)\n3. **kv-pair parsing duplication** (hub loses schema validation)\n4. **Role-field resolution duplication** (consumers parse TSV directly)\n5. **UI seam scope entanglement** (new commands can't import UI cleanly)\n6. **Provider contract verification** (silent runtime failure on malformed adapters)\n7. **Status-to-opts round-trip untested** (schema drift silent)\n8. **Schema registry fragmentation** (readers hardcode field lists)\n\nNone of these are blockers — the codebase is already well-modularized. These are refinements to **deepen the seams that are already declared** in CONTEXT.md: each would save a few dozen lines at the next loop/provider addition and forestall off-by-one schema bugs."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"92d47660-4965-47f1-a4d3-fc4dbbc15bee","uuid":"828e00c1-ddba-4ad8-b342-18041f446e8b","timestamp":"2026-05-27T06:32:14.799Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are doing an architectural friction survey for the Almanac codebase, a Bash-based loop engine. The domain model is already documented at `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/CONTEXT.md` — read it first; it names the seams.\n\nGoal: find SHALLOW MODULES, leaky seams, missing locality, hard-to-test surfaces. Apply the deletion test: if I deleted this module, would complexity disappear (pass-through) or reconverge in one place (earning its keep)?\n\nConcretely walk through:\n- `lib/core.sh`, `lib/almanac-core.sh`, `lib/ui.sh`, `lib/role.sh`, `lib/agent.sh`, `lib/feedback.sh`, `lib/loops.sh`, `lib/run.sh`, `lib/worker.sh`, `lib/loop-launcher.sh`, `lib/harden-core.sh`, `lib/converge-core.sh`\n- `lib/loops/{ralph,harden,converge}.sh` — do they share boilerplate that should sit behind a deeper seam?\n- `lib/providers/{claude,codex}.sh` — same question\n- `cmd/*.sh` — are these thin dispatchers or do they have surprising logic?\n- `tests/*.sh` — what's hard to test? What's tested via shell mock gymnastics instead of through a clean interface?\n\nFor each friction point, report:\n1. **Location** — file paths + line ranges\n2. **Symptom** — what feels off (shallow interface, duplicated invocation patterns, missing seam, leaky abstraction, fragile test)\n3. **Deletion test** — would removing this concentrate or just move complexity?\n4. **Candidate deepening** — one-sentence hint at what the deep module would own\n\nDon't propose interfaces yet — just identify the friction. Be specific (cite line numbers). 5–8 candidates, ranked by severity. Under 1200 words.\n\nDon't list things that are already good — `agent.sh`'s three-shape split, the provider auto-discovery, the launcher/hub split, the slug centralization, and the generic TSV record engine are explicitly called out in CONTEXT.md as deep — skip those.","agentId":"abd51fed17ab40d18","agentType"